{% comment %}
  AI Visibility - app embed block.

  Static markup only: no JavaScript, no fetch, no external requests. Everything
  needed is already in the metafields at render time (PRD §5.2). Built for
  Shopify allows no more than a 10 point storefront performance impact, and the
  cheapest way to guarantee that is to ship nothing that executes.

  Two modes, chosen by the merchant after we detect what the theme already does:
    extend  - leave the theme's own Product node alone, and emit our complete
              node only when the theme has none. Default.
    full    - emit a complete Product node, for themes with none of their own.

  Structured data here describes only what the page shows (Google: "Don't mark
  up content that is not visible to readers of the page",
  https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/sd-policies).
  What the app extracts - summary, facts, who it suits, buyer questions - is
  printed as visible text by the content blocks (blocks/ai-visibility-content,
  blocks/product-content), and FAQPage is emitted there, next to the questions
  it describes. This head block cannot see those blocks' settings, so since 11
  September 2026 it publishes none of the extracted values itself.
{% endcomment %}

{%- if block.settings.enabled -%}
  {%- assign av_business = shop.metafields['$app'].business.value -%}
  {%- assign av_org_id = shop.metafields['$app'].theme_scan.value.organizationId -%}
  {%- assign av_theme_product_id = shop.metafields['$app'].theme_scan.value.productId -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The @id our own Organization node carries when we emit one below. The
    theme scan recognises this suffix as ours and excludes it when deciding
    whether the theme emits an Organization node, so our own output can
    never feed back into the scan as "the theme has one". If the mirrored
    organizationId ever is our own id (an old scan recorded before that
    exclusion), extending it would produce an orphan @id-only fragment, so
    it is cleared and we emit our complete node instead.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_own_org_id = shop.url | append: '#organization' -%}
  {%- if av_org_id == av_own_org_id -%}
    {%- assign av_org_id = "" -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Operator-only switch, set during a paid setup engagement (never a
    merchant-facing toggle). Gates a handful of extra schema.org properties
    that are correct to publish but were absent from the general release.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_seo_unlocked = shop.metafields['$app'].seo_unlocked.value -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Computed at render time, never stored: "today" moves every day the page is
    rendered, so a stored date would eventually be in the past. One year out
    is the conventional ceiling for priceValidUntil. This is a synthetic
    date - no merchant states it anywhere - and it is accepted anyway
    because it is computed, industry-standard, and recommended by Google;
    it claims nothing factual about the product itself. itemCondition, by
    contrast, was removed: publishing NewCondition unconditionally was a
    factual claim the merchant never made, false for refurbished or
    second-hand goods, and there is no condition field to derive it from.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_price_valid_until = "now" | date: "%s" | plus: 31536000 | date: "%Y-%m-%d" -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Ratings come from review apps' semi-standard metafields (Judge.me and
    others write reviews.rating / reviews.rating_count). Published only when
    real reviews exist - a shop without reviews gets no rating markup at all.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_rating = product.metafields.reviews.rating.value -%}
  {%- assign av_rating_count = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value | default: 0 -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Delivery, as numbers (CC-PROMPT-AI-READABILITY-4 item 2). The Business
    screen's cost text is read on save into deliveryCostParsed (rate,
    currency, freeOverAmount; delivery-parse.ts); the text itself stays the
    merchant's own everywhere text is published. A record saved before that
    reading existed has none, and publishes no delivery price until the next
    save - never a figure guessed at render time.

    A rate is publishable only when the "This is a starting price" box is not
    ticked. Google's shippingRate is one MonetaryAmount with one value
    (https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/merchant-listing:
    "You can only specify one shippingRate per OfferShippingDetails
    property"), so a starting price published as the rate would be false for
    every order that costs more.

    Destination: the countries typed on the Business screen, else the shop's
    own country. DefinedRegion takes "The two-letter country code, in ISO
    3166-1 alpha-2 format" (same page). Captured once, as JSON built by the
    json filter, because it is printed in more than one node.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_ship = av_business.deliveryCostParsed -%}
  {%- assign av_ship_rate_ok = false -%}
  {%- if av_ship.rate != nil and av_business.deliveryCostIsFrom != true -%}
    {%- assign av_ship_rate_ok = true -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- assign av_ship_countries = av_business.deliveryCountries -%}
  {%- if av_ship_countries == blank and shop.address.country_code != blank -%}
    {%- assign av_ship_countries = shop.address.country_code | split: "," -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- capture av_ship_destination -%}
    {%- if av_ship_countries.size == 1 -%}
      { "@type": "DefinedRegion", "addressCountry": {{ av_ship_countries.first | json }} }
    {%- elsif av_ship_countries.size > 1 -%}
      [{%- for av_country in av_ship_countries -%}{ "@type": "DefinedRegion", "addressCountry": {{ av_country | json }} }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}{%- endfor -%}]
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endcapture -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The shop's delivery policy, on the Organization node below
    (CC-PROMPT-AI-READABILITY-4 item 3). Google recommends it over per-offer
    details: "We recommend you provide a global shipping policy for your
    business under Organization markup instead" (merchant listing
    documentation, shippingDetails), as a ShippingService "nested under the
    Organization structured data type using the hasShippingService property"
    (https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/shipping-policy).

    ShippingService requires shippingConditions, so it exists only when there
    is a condition to state:
      - the base rate, when a rate was read and the starting price box is not
        ticked - the rule of item 2d, for the same reason;
      - free delivery from the threshold, when one was read.
    With both, the base condition is bounded by order value below the
    threshold, as the documentation's own example bounds its 3.49 rate with
    "maxValue": 29.99 and starts its free condition at "minValue": 30. A
    threshold read from "over 200" and from "from 200" alike starts the free
    condition at 200 and ends the base one a cent below.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_ship_free = false -%}
  {%- if av_ship.freeOverAmount != nil -%}
    {%- assign av_ship_free = true -%}
    {%- assign av_ship_free_below = av_ship.freeOverAmount | times: 100 | minus: 1 | divided_by: 100.0 -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- assign av_ship_service = false -%}
  {%- if av_ship_rate_ok or av_ship_free -%}
    {%- assign av_ship_service = true -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- assign av_ship_service_id = shop.url | append: '/#shipping' -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The delivery time in whole days (CC-PROMPT-AI-READABILITY-4 item 4), read
    on save into deliveryTimeParsed. It used to go out as transitTimeLabel
    inside ShippingDeliveryTime, which schema.org never allowed there (its
    domain was OfferShippingDetails, https://schema.org/transitTimeLabel) and
    has since retired. The merchant listing documentation reads a
    transitTime QuantitativeValue: "A minimum and maximum number of days must
    be specified", unitCode "DAY or d". Captured once, printed in the
    policy's conditions and on the Offer.

    No businessDays, even for "working days": the merchant listing
    documentation lists only handlingTime and transitTime under
    ShippingDeliveryTime, and minValue, maxValue and unitCode under its
    QuantitativeValue. "Delivery time varies by product" publishes none.
    A record saved before this reading existed has none, and publishes no
    delivery time until the next save.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_days = av_business.deliveryTimeParsed -%}
  {%- assign av_ship_days = false -%}
  {%- if av_days.maxDays != nil and av_business.deliveryVaries != true -%}
    {%- assign av_ship_days = true -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- capture av_days_qv -%}
    { "@type": "QuantitativeValue", "minValue": {{ av_days.minDays | json }}, "maxValue": {{ av_days.maxDays | json }}, "unitCode": "DAY" }
  {%- endcapture -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Official store profiles (sameAs), site-wide. No spec for social networks
    existed until this feature; the merchant states them once on the
    Business info screen, we never verify a profile exists, and we publish
    nothing at all when none are filled in - an empty sameAs is worse than
    no node.

    We extend the theme's own Organization node only when the scan found a
    real @id to reference. An identifier we invented, or a node the theme
    has but that carries no @id of its own, gives us nothing to attach to,
    so every other case gets our own complete minimal node, carrying its
    own stable @id.

    When the theme emits an id-less Organization node of its own, the page
    then carries two Organization nodes. Decided 1 September 2026: that is
    acceptable - it is not a policy violation, and consumers merge or pick.
    The earlier rule suppressed our node in this case to keep the conflict
    count at zero, which meant the merchant's own profiles vanished from
    structured data so that our counter read cleaner - publishing less in
    order to measure better, the wrong trade. The SEO screen reports this
    pair as informational, never as a defect.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_social = av_business.socialProfiles -%}
  {%- assign av_same_as = "" -%}
  {%- if av_social.facebook != blank -%}{% assign av_same_as = av_same_as | append: av_social.facebook | append: "|" %}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if av_social.instagram != blank -%}{% assign av_same_as = av_same_as | append: av_social.instagram | append: "|" %}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if av_social.tiktok != blank -%}{% assign av_same_as = av_same_as | append: av_social.tiktok | append: "|" %}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if av_social.youtube != blank -%}{% assign av_same_as = av_same_as | append: av_social.youtube | append: "|" %}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if av_social.linkedin != blank -%}{% assign av_same_as = av_same_as | append: av_social.linkedin | append: "|" %}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if av_social.x != blank -%}{% assign av_same_as = av_same_as | append: av_social.x | append: "|" %}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if av_social.pinterest != blank -%}{% assign av_same_as = av_same_as | append: av_social.pinterest | append: "|" %}{%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    uniq, because the same profile can legitimately be typed into more than
    one field, and a sameAs listing one address five times tells a search
    engine less than one listing it once. Seen in production on 31 August
    2026: five identical Facebook URLs on a live product page.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_same_as_list = av_same_as | split: "|" | uniq -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Emitted for the store's profiles, its delivery policy, or both; nothing
    at all when neither is stated. Every field after the address carries a
    leading comma, so no combination of present and absent parts can trail.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if av_same_as_list.size > 0 or av_ship_service -%}
    <script type="application/ld+json">
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "Organization",
        {%- comment -%}
          Our emitter marker. The server reads this and nothing else to tell our
          nodes from the theme's: an @id cannot carry it, because in extend mode
          our node deliberately shares the theme's address so the two merge into
          one node. An absolute IRI as a key is valid JSON-LD, needs no
          @context term, is ignored by consumers that do not know it, and
          pollutes no schema.org property. See conflicts.ts, OUR_NODE_MARKER.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        "https://mrdigital.ro/ns/ai-visibility": "1",
        {%- if av_org_id != blank -%}
        "@id": {{ av_org_id | json }}
        {%- else -%}
        "@id": {{ av_own_org_id | json }},
        "name": {{ shop.name | json }},
        "url": {{ shop.url | json }}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if av_same_as_list.size > 0 %}
        ,"sameAs": [
          {%- for url in av_same_as_list -%}
            {{ url | json }}{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
          {%- endfor -%}
        ]
        {%- endif %}
        {%- comment -%}
          Property by property as the shipping policy documentation's example
          states them: ShippingService with its @id and name, then one
          ShippingConditions per case, each with shippingDestination,
          orderValue where a threshold bounds it, and shippingRate. The name is
          the merchant's own delivery cost text. Each Offer refers to this
          service by its @id (see the Offer's shippingDetails).
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if av_ship_service %}
        ,"hasShippingService": {
          "@type": "ShippingService",
          "@id": {{ av_ship_service_id | json }},
          "name": {{ av_business.deliveryCost | json }},
          "shippingConditions": [
            {%- if av_ship_rate_ok %}
            {
              "@type": "ShippingConditions"
              {%- if av_ship_countries.size > 0 %}
              ,"shippingDestination": {{ av_ship_destination }}
              {%- endif %}
              {%- if av_ship_free %}
              ,"orderValue": { "@type": "MonetaryAmount", "minValue": 0, "maxValue": {{ av_ship_free_below | json }}, "currency": {{ av_ship.currency | json }} }
              {%- endif %}
              ,"shippingRate": { "@type": "MonetaryAmount", "value": {{ av_ship.rate | json }}, "currency": {{ av_ship.currency | json }} }
              {%- if av_ship_days %}
              ,"transitTime": { "@type": "ServicePeriod", "duration": {{ av_days_qv }} }
              {%- endif %}
            }
            {%- endif %}
            {%- if av_ship_rate_ok and av_ship_free %},{% endif %}
            {%- if av_ship_free %}
            {
              "@type": "ShippingConditions"
              {%- if av_ship_countries.size > 0 %}
              ,"shippingDestination": {{ av_ship_destination }}
              {%- endif %}
              ,"orderValue": { "@type": "MonetaryAmount", "minValue": {{ av_ship.freeOverAmount | json }}, "currency": {{ av_ship.currency | json }} }
              ,"shippingRate": { "@type": "MonetaryAmount", "value": 0, "currency": {{ av_ship.currency | json }} }
              {%- if av_ship_days %}
              ,"transitTime": { "@type": "ServicePeriod", "duration": {{ av_days_qv }} }
              {%- endif %}
            }
            {%- endif %}
          ]
        }
        {%- endif %}
      }
    </script>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    WebSite + SearchAction, home page only (template.name 'index'). Emitting
    this on every page would be one node repeated on every request for no
    reason; a search engine or assistant only needs it once, from the page
    that represents the site as a whole.

    And only when the theme has none of its own (theme_scan.hasWebSiteLd
    false; PRD-AI-READABILITY P0.4). A page is meant to carry one WebSite
    node; Shella and most themes already emit theirs, and until 11 September
    2026 ours went out beside it, with no marker, so the SEO screen reported
    the pair as "unknown source". It carries the marker now, which is what
    keeps the scan from reading it back as the theme's and switching it off
    on the next pass. A theme_scan written before the flag existed holds
    ours back until a scan says the theme has none, for the same reason the
    Product node does: the duplicate is the thing to avoid.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign av_theme_has_website = shop.metafields['$app'].theme_scan.value.hasWebSiteLd -%}
  {%- if template.name == 'index' and av_seo_unlocked and av_theme_has_website == false -%}
    <script type="application/ld+json">
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "WebSite",
        {%- comment -%} Our emitter marker - see the Organization node above. {%- endcomment -%}
        "https://mrdigital.ro/ns/ai-visibility": "1",
        "url": {{ shop.url | json }},
        "potentialAction": {
          "@type": "SearchAction",
          "target": {
            "@type": "EntryPoint",
            {%- comment -%}
              The placeholder stays outside the Liquid tag on purpose. A
              literal brace inside `{{ ... }}` makes Liquid's lexer end the
              tag early, which fails the bundle at `shopify app deploy` with
              "not properly terminated" - the only place Liquid is parsed.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            "urlTemplate": "{{ shop.url }}/search?q={search_term_string}"
          },
          "query-input": "required name=search_term_string"
        }
      }
    </script>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if template.name == 'product' and product -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Which Product node this page gets from us. Full mode always emits the
      complete node. Extend mode reads the theme scan and has three cases:

      1. The theme's Product node carries an @id (theme_scan.productId set):
         the theme's node stands and we add nothing to it. Until 11 September
         2026 this case emitted a fragment under the theme's @id carrying the
         summary, who it suits and the facts; all three are now visible text
         in the body, and structured data from this block describes only what
         the page shows, so the fragment had nothing left to say.
      2. The theme has a Product node with no @id (hasProductLd true,
         productId blank): hold back, emit no Product node at all. There is
         nothing to attach a fragment to - one under an invented @id is an
         orphan read as a second product - and our complete node beside the
         theme's would be two complete Product nodes, the thing this app
         refuses outright. The SEO screen says so (B33).
      3. The theme has no Product node (hasProductLd false): emit the
         complete node exactly as full mode does. Nothing on the page can
         collide with it, and without it the page has no Product node at
         all. This is also what clears B33: once the merchant turns the
         theme's own product schema off, the next scan records false and
         this branch takes over with no switch to flip.

      A theme_scan written before hasProductLd existed carries no flag at
      all, which is neither true nor false here, and falls into case 2 - the
      behaviour the block had before this rule. Case 3 needs a scan that
      says so, because guessing it wrong on a theme like Shella would print
      the second complete node.

      The mirror link, the llms.txt link and the snippet directive below are
      unaffected: they are not schema and cannot collide.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign av_theme_has_product = shop.metafields['$app'].theme_scan.value.hasProductLd -%}
    {%- assign av_product_ld = 'full' -%}
    {%- if block.settings.mode == 'extend' -%}
      {%- if av_theme_product_id != blank -%}
        {%- assign av_product_ld = 'extend' -%}
      {%- elsif av_theme_has_product == false -%}
        {%- assign av_product_ld = 'full' -%}
      {%- else -%}
        {%- assign av_product_ld = 'none' -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if av_product_ld == 'full' -%}
      <script type="application/ld+json">
        {
          "@context": "https://schema.org",
          "@type": "Product",
          "@id": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | append: '#product' | json }},
          {%- comment -%} Our emitter marker - see the Organization node above. {%- endcomment -%}
          "https://mrdigital.ro/ns/ai-visibility": "1",
          "name": {{ product.title | json }},
          {%- comment -%}
            The theme's own product description, which the page shows. Never
            the summary: this block cannot know whether the body shows it.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          "description": {{ product.description | strip_html | truncate: 500 | json }},
          "url": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }},
          {%- if product.featured_image %}
          "image": {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 1200 | prepend: 'https:' | json }},
          {%- endif %}
          {%- if product.vendor != blank %}
          "brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": {{ product.vendor | json }} },
          {%- endif %}
          {%- if product.selected_or_first_available_variant.sku != blank %}
          "sku": {{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.sku | json }},
          {%- endif %}
          {%- if product.selected_or_first_available_variant.barcode != blank %}
          "gtin": {{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.barcode | json }},
          {%- endif %}
          {%- if av_rating and av_rating_count > 0 %}
          "aggregateRating": {
            "@type": "AggregateRating",
            "ratingValue": {{ av_rating.value | default: av_rating | json }},
            "reviewCount": {{ av_rating_count | json }}
          },
          {%- endif %}
          {%- if product.price_varies %}
          {%- comment -%}
            A variable product publishes its real span, not one figure that
            is wrong for every variant but one (WP 1.6.7).

            No shippingDetails here (CC-PROMPT-AI-READABILITY-4 item 2e).
            The merchant listing documentation lists shippingDetails among
            the Offer's properties only, and says of offers: "Product snippets
            accept an Offer or AggregateOffer but merchant listings require
            an Offer". An AggregateOffer is therefore outside the markup that
            reads shipping details, and the shop-wide shipping policy on the
            Organization node covers these products too.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          "offers": {
            "@type": "AggregateOffer",
            "lowPrice": {{ product.price_min | divided_by: 100.0 | json }},
            "highPrice": {{ product.price_max | divided_by: 100.0 | json }},
            "priceCurrency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }},
            "offerCount": {{ product.variants.size | json }},
            "availability": {%- if product.available %} "https://schema.org/InStock"{% else %} "https://schema.org/OutOfStock"{% endif %},
            {%- if av_seo_unlocked %}
            "priceValidUntil": {{ av_price_valid_until | json }},
            {%- endif %}
            "url": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }}
          }
          {%- else %}
          "offers": {
            "@type": "Offer",
            "price": {{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.price | divided_by: 100.0 | json }},
            "priceCurrency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }},
            "availability": {%- if product.available %} "https://schema.org/InStock"{% else %} "https://schema.org/OutOfStock"{% endif %},
            {%- if av_seo_unlocked %}
            "priceValidUntil": {{ av_price_valid_until | json }},
            {%- endif %}
            {%- comment -%}
              When the shop-wide policy exists (item 3), the Offer refers to it
              and states nothing else. The merchant listing documentation: "To
              unambiguously reference your global shipping policy (located on
              a different page) from an Offer, use only the hasShippingService
              property under the OfferShippingDetails type using only the @id
              keyword", and offer-level details are for the exception: "Only if
              some of your products have specific shipping policies for which
              you need to override your global shipping policy, or if you don't
              provide a standard shipping policy for your business, use this
              property under Offer." The documentation does not require the
              Offer to keep its own fields beside the reference, and Google
              ranks "Product-level merchant listing markup" above
              "Organization-level markup" (shipping policy documentation, order
              of precedence), so fields kept here would override the policy
              they duplicate. The rate, destination and threshold of item 2d
              are therefore on the policy, and the visitor's currency no longer
              matters here: no rate is ever stated on the Offer.

              With no shop-wide policy - no rate that can be published and no
              threshold - the Offer states what item 2d still allows:
              destination and delivery time, never a rate (the starting price
              box, or no price read). "Delivery time varies by product"
              publishes no delivery time, and then nothing. Every field after
              the type carries a leading comma.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if av_ship_service %}
            "shippingDetails": {
              "@type": "OfferShippingDetails",
              "hasShippingService": { "@id": {{ av_ship_service_id | json }} }
            },
            {%- elsif av_ship_days %}
            "shippingDetails": {
              "@type": "OfferShippingDetails"
              {%- if av_ship_countries.size > 0 %}
              ,"shippingDestination": {{ av_ship_destination }}
              {%- endif %}
              ,"deliveryTime": { "@type": "ShippingDeliveryTime", "transitTime": {{ av_days_qv }} }
            },
            {%- endif %}
            {%- if av_business.returnDays %}
            "hasMerchantReturnPolicy": {
              "@type": "MerchantReturnPolicy",
              "returnPolicyCategory": "https://schema.org/MerchantReturnFiniteReturnWindow",
              "merchantReturnDays": {{ av_business.returnDays | json }}
            },
            {%- endif %}
            "url": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }}
          }
          {%- endif %}
        }
      </script>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      No FAQPage here. It is emitted by the content snippet
      (snippets/ai-visibility-content.liquid) right after the questions it
      describes, and only when they are shown: this block cannot see whether
      the merchant switched the questions off, so emitting it here marked up
      text that might not be on the page (11 September 2026).
    {%- endcomment -%}

    {%- if av_seo_unlocked -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        BreadcrumbList. Built from the collection the visitor arrived through
        when Liquid exposes it (a product reached via
        /collections/x/products/y carries that collection in context here);
        falls back to the product's own first collection otherwise. Position
        1 is always the home page, positions are contiguous.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign av_bc_collection = collection -%}
      {%- if av_bc_collection == blank -%}
        {%- assign av_bc_collection = product.collections.first -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      <script type="application/ld+json">
        {
          "@context": "https://schema.org",
          "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
          "itemListElement": [
            { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": {{ shop.name | json }}, "item": {{ shop.url | json }} }
            {%- if av_bc_collection != blank -%}
            ,{ "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": {{ av_bc_collection.title | json }}, "item": {{ shop.url | append: av_bc_collection.url | json }} }
            {%- endif -%}
            ,{ "@type": "ListItem", "position": {% if av_bc_collection != blank %}3{% else %}2{% endif %}, "name": {{ product.title | json }}, "item": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }} }
          ]
        }
      </script>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if block.settings.mirror -%}
      {%- comment -%} Discovery link for the plain text mirror. {%- endcomment -%}
      <link rel="alternate" type="text/plain"
            href="{{ shop.url }}/apps/ai-visibility/{{ product.handle }}"
            title="Plain text version">
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Collection pages (PRD §4.8). A listing page is where an assistant answers
    "what kinds of X are there"; a grid of thumbnails answers nothing. This is
    a CollectionPage, not a Product node, so it never collides with the
    theme's product markup.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if template.name == 'collection' and collection -%}
    {%- assign av_c_summary = collection.metafields['$app'].summary.value -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The node exists for collections the app has processed (a summary was
      written), and describes what the page shows: the collection's own
      title and description, and its products. The summary, the criteria and
      the questions are visible text from the content embed, which also
      emits the FAQPage; this block cannot see that embed's settings.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign av_c_desc = collection.description | strip_html | strip | truncate: 500 -%}
    {%- if av_c_summary != blank -%}
      <script type="application/ld+json">
        {
          "@context": "https://schema.org",
          "@type": "CollectionPage",
          "@id": {{ shop.url | append: collection.url | append: '#collection' | json }},
          {%- comment -%} Our emitter marker - see the Organization node above. {%- endcomment -%}
          "https://mrdigital.ro/ns/ai-visibility": "1",
          "name": {{ collection.title | json }},
          {%- if av_c_desc != blank %}
          "description": {{ av_c_desc | json }},
          {%- endif %}
          "url": {{ shop.url | append: collection.url | json }},
          "mainEntity": {
            "@type": "ItemList",
            "numberOfItems": {{ collection.products_count | json }},
            "itemListElement": [
              {%- for p in collection.products limit: 60 -%}
                {
                  "@type": "ListItem",
                  "position": {{ forloop.index }},
                  "name": {{ p.title | json }},
                  "url": {{ shop.url | append: p.url | json }}
                }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
              {%- endfor -%}
            ]
          }
        }
      </script>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The llms.txt proposal names rel="describedby" as the way a page points
    at the llms.txt covering it. This app's llms.txt is generated live under
    the app proxy path; the bare /llms.txt route is not something this app
    serves, so pointing there risked a 404. Same target as the Link header
    the plain text mirror sends.

    Shop-wide on purpose, not product-only (moved 11 September 2026): a
    crawler landing on the home page or a collection page never saw this
    link when it lived inside the product-only block, so nothing on the
    storefront pointed at the mirrors except this one link, and only on
    product pages. Needs no product data, so it belongs at the top level
    with the Organization and WebSite nodes above, not inside the
    product-only section. Our own file, no theme file touched.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if block.settings.llms_link -%}
    <link rel="describedby" href="{{ shop.url }}/apps/ai-visibility/llms.txt">
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if block.settings.lift_snippets -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      One line, outsized effect: assistants may quote the page in full rather
      than a truncated snippet. Ported from the WordPress module.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <meta name="robots" content="max-snippet:-1, max-image-preview:large, max-video-preview:-1">
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "AI Visibility",
  "target": "head",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enabled",
      "label": "Enable AI Visibility output",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "mode",
      "label": "Structured data mode",
      "info": "Extend is safe on themes that already emit a Product node. Use Full only if your theme emits none.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "extend", "label": "Extend the theme's Product data" },
        { "value": "full", "label": "Emit a complete Product node" }
      ],
      "default": "extend"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "mirror",
      "label": "Link to the plain text version",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "llms_link",
      "label": "Point crawlers at your llms.txt",
      "info": "Adds a standard describedby link to the llms.txt this app generates live for your store.",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "lift_snippets",
      "label": "Allow assistants to quote the full page",
      "info": "Adds max-snippet:-1, so answers can cite you properly instead of a truncated line.",
      "default": true
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
